{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-7311eef5-ad0c-430f-802b-9e9c0b71fa9c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab743d1be157ae9e","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-8cwhzp0v/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (branch B)\n\n## Description\n\nConditional branch, executed only if the provisioning go/no-go decision selects BFF-managed provisioning — i.e., the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5, and the BFF-managed alternative is judged sound at the gate. The gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, similar to how it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret. This preserves the out-of-the-box experience without waiting for the operator layer, at the cost of explicit lifecycle design attention because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop.\n\n## Scope\n\n- gen-ai BFF logic that creates the pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance (the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image, `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` — pgvector included, no custom image build) as Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace\n- Idempotent provisioning: an existing suitable instance in the namespace is reused, never duplicated\n- Explicit lifecycle design and implementation for a non-operator controller: cleanup on playground deletion, behavior on pod restarts, and credential rotation\n- Portability by design: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning\n- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values, adjusted per benchmark findings\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG causes a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) to be created in the namespace when none exists — no manual user provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure\n- Provisioning is idempotent: a second RAG-enabled playground in the same namespace reuses the existing instance rather than creating another\n- Cleanup on playground deletion is implemented and verified; behavior on pod restarts is defined and verified (no silent data loss or orphaned instances); credential rotation is handled or explicitly documented as out of scope with rationale\n- The provisioning code path is designed for portability: consuming a platform-provided shared connection string instead of self-provisioning requires a configuration change, not a rework\n- Resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values as the starting point\n- The provisioned instance is scoped per namespace (multi-tenant isolation between namespaces)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\": this branch delivers the out-of-the-box provisioning mechanism without the operator layer.\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — \"Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability\": this branch supplies the instance the wired provider path runs against.\n- **HLR-4 (P1)** — \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation)\": delivered by the per-namespace provisioning scope.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":