{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5f515836e01ca3f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n4\t  (branch B)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Dashboard Crimson Scrum\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: B\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: If the go/no-go decision selects operator-based provisioning\n17\t    (RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time) or judges the BFF-managed lifecycle risks unmanageable,\n18\t    this epic is not executed; provisioning happens in the operator layer or customers\n19\t    bring their own PostgreSQL per the decision record.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: -1\n26\t  external_dependency: 0\n27\t  human_process_gates: 0\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 5\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\t---\n36\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (branch B)\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tConditional branch, executed only if the provisioning go/no-go decision selects BFF-managed provisioning — i.e., the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5, and the BFF-managed alternative is judged sound at the gate. The gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, similar to how it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret. This preserves the out-of-the-box experience without waiting for the operator layer, at the cost of explicit lifecycle design attention because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- gen-ai BFF logic that creates the pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance (the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image, `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` — pgvector included, no custom image build) as Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace\n45\t- Idempotent provisioning: an existing suitable instance in the namespace is reused, never duplicated\n46\t- Explicit lifecycle design and implementation for a non-operator controller: cleanup on playground deletion, behavior on pod restarts, and credential rotation\n47\t- Portability by design: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning\n48\t- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values, adjusted per benchmark findings\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- Configuring a playground with RAG causes a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) to be created in the namespace when none exists — no manual user provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure\n53\t- Provisioning is idempotent: a second RAG-enabled playground in the same namespace reuses the existing instance rather than creating another\n54\t- Cleanup on playground deletion is implemented and verified; behavior on pod restarts is defined and verified (no silent data loss or orphaned instances); credential rotation is handled or explicitly documented as out of scope with rationale\n55\t- The provisioning code path is designed for portability: consuming a platform-provided shared connection string instead of self-provisioning requires a configuration change, not a rework\n56\t- Resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values as the starting point\n57\t- The provisioned instance is scoped per namespace (multi-tenant isolation between namespaces)\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\": this branch delivers the out-of-the-box provisioning mechanism without the operator layer.\n62\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — \"Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability\": this branch supplies the instance the wired provider path runs against.\n63\t- **HLR-4 (P1)** — \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation)\": delivered by the per-namespace provisioning scope.\n64\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ada7191bb267446e","agent_id":"a36f22da151b040d7","session_id":"9929ed70-9f3a-43fc-bc5e-5275c2fe8952","uuid":"0a7dc29f-e4ed-4786-9242-16992fddd221","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:37:52.312Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5f515836e01ca3f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
