six derived HLRs (HLR-1..HLR-6) map to epics, with the traceability matrix recording conditional branch coverage for HLR-1/HLR-2. All three P0 HLRs are covered by main-plan plus branch epics. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) gates the P0 branches; E002 (P0) blocks only E004 (P1); no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work. No docs-authoring epic exists, so no exemption question arises.\n34\t\n35\tPriority collapse examined and not found: HLR-4 (P1, per-namespace isolation) and HLR-6 (P2, portability) are bundled into E002 (P0) with `bundled` flags and rationale in the matrix rows (validator-confirmed). Neither is a distinct deferrable feature — per-namespace isolation is intrinsic to the same OGXServer/provider configuration surface (deferring it would ship cross-tenant data sharing, a regression, not a deferrable increment), and HLR-6 is a design constraint (abstracted connection-details source) on the same surface, not separate functionality. The provisioning branches carry instance-side scoping as P0 work.\n36\t\n37\tThe HLR list itself is derived from top-level Acceptance Criteria because the strategy attachment was unavailable — the decomposition flags this prominently in Health Warnings with a re-verify instruction, which is the correct handling of an input limitation.\n38\t\n39\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n40\t\n41\tCross-check passed in both directions: every diagram edge (E001→BRANCH-A/B/C-E003, E002→E004) has a matching frontmatter `dependencies` entry in the target epic, and vice versa. Arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention. No cycles, no edges to nonexistent epics.\n42\t\n43\tConditional branch handling is correct: each branch file sets `branch` (A/B/C, matching filename) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, and `gated_by` targets appear in each branch's direct `dependencies`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies`.\n44\t\n45\tImplementation-type parallelism (Rules 7/8/10): no `repo-onboarding`, `konflux-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics exist — correctly eliminated per Staff Engineer Input Correction 2 (existing pgvector-enabled Red Hat PostgreSQL image, model-registry-operator precedent, CSV env var already present). No parallel-rule violations possible.\n46\t\n47\tDeliberately omitted edges examined and confirmed rule-compliant: no E001→E002 edge and no branch→E002 edge. The provisioned database's connection details are a configuration reference consumed as content — Rule 13 requires an AC gate, not a DAG edge, and E002 carries exactly that AC (\"verified against an instance provisioned by the provisioning approach selected at the go/no-go decision\"). Per the review rules, the absence of such edges is the compliant shape, not a missing dependency. The branch epics' \"resource requests/limits ... adjusted per benchmark findings\" reference to E004's output is likewise a content reference with an independently testable starting point (model-registry-operator reference values), not a missing edge.\n48\t\n49\tCritical path length 2 (E002→E004; E001→selected branch is also 2) is reasonable for the post-reduction scope: staff input eliminated the 1–2 sprint image-onboarding chain, and the vector_io provider already ships in the distribution.\n50\t\n51\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n52\t\n53\tAll epics use canonical component \"Gen AI Studio\" and team \"Dashboard Crimson Scrum\" (inferred from the strategy label, flagged in Health Warnings with a planning-verification note for branch A's possible team shift — acceptable given the missing Impacted Teams attachment). No epic spans multiple components or teams.\n54\t\n55\tThe three branch epics are mutually exclusive conditional alternatives selected by one gate — not scope duplication; each implements a different provisioning mechanism on a different surface (operator layer / BFF / CR-spec configuration). E002 (wiring) vs. branches (provisioning) is a clean separation of the two work streams Staff Engineer Input distinguishes (\"the majority of the work effort is around infrastructure and provisioning... not the vector_io provider integration itself\").\n56\t\n57\tSize: no epic approaches multi-sprint scope. E002 is one logical change on one configuration surface; branch B's lifecycle design work is bounded by its enumerated ACs.\n58\t\n59\tWork-product type mixing examined: branch C includes \"customer-facing configuration guidance\" alongside implementation, but it is explicitly scoped as thin config-path documentation (\"no new documentation topics are authored\") supporting the feature's own AC — not substantive content authoring like notebooks or tutorials. Acceptable as in-scope; the decomposition's Rule 11 justification for no docs-authoring epic is coherent.\n60\t\n61\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n62\t\n63\tE001 (Investigation) genuinely changes downstream structure: its outcome selects which of three branch epics exists — the strongest possible case for Investigation typing. E002, E004, and the branches are Implementation and produce concrete artifacts (provider wiring, benchmark/findings report, provisioning mechanisms).\n64\t\n65\tGating metadata is complete and correct: all three branch epics have `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001` present in their direct `dependencies`, and `gate_failure_impact` with both `action: remove` and a substantive `fallback_approach`. No main-plan epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by` (E002/E004 deliberately do not depend on E001, with a recorded Rule 13 justification).\n66\t\n67\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n68\t\n69\tType-appropriate signal sets are used everywhere: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); all five Implementation epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). No cross-type contamination. Frontmatter values match the ai-signals rationale tables exactly for all six epics (verified signal-by-signal).\n70\t\n71\tE001's investigation signals are well calibrated: `source_accessibility: 0` correctly reflects that the gating answer (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery commitment) is a pending human/team decision, not a readable record, and `human_judgment_required: -2` matches the sprint-planning go/no-go gate Staff Engineer Input mandates.\n72\t\n73\t`architecture_claims` calibration verified against `.context`: the cited files exist (`odh-dashboard.md` — gen-ai Go BFF sidecar confirmed; `ogx-distribution.md` — PGVector content confirmed; `model-registry-operator.md`, `rhods-operator.md`, `ogx-k8s-operator.md`), and the epics shaped by the strategy's architecture premises (remote pgvector backend shipped, BFF configures OGXServer instances) score 1 with citations — correctly calibrated, not the \"no architecture claims\" miss.\n74\t\n75\tBranch signal differentiation is sound: branch A scores `existing_foundation: 0` / `open_questions: -1` / `external_dependency: -1` / `repo_access: 0` (target operator layer does not exist yet), while branches B/C score higher on their concrete, staff-specified mechanisms — matching the actual risk profile of each outcome.\n76\t\n77\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n78\t\n79\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy and staff input. Rule-mandated ACs: E002 (the replacement epic — remote provider replacing the inline provider path) carries the rollback/feature-flag AC (\"Provider selection remains configurable, so the inline provider path can be restored as a rollback/fallback\"); no konflux-chain or docs-authoring epics exist, so those mandates are n/a.\n80\t\n81\tNon-testable AC scan: no AC states an externally-controlled precondition (no lab turnaround, procurement, or vendor-timing conditions). E001's \"downstream provisioning scope... confirmed as executable within the release timeline\" is a condition on the epic's own deliverable (the decision record's content), not an externally-granted availability, so it is a valid AC. The RHAISTRAT-7164 timing risk is correctly handled as a gate with fallback, not misfiled as an AC.\n82\t\n83\tNo draft-ID references in epic bodies (grep-verified): bodies reference \"the provisioning go/no-go decision\" and full Jira keys, never bare \"E00x\" sibling IDs.\n84\t\n85\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n86\t\n87\tAll strategy-level acceptance criteria are accounted for: out-of-the-box availability (E001 + branches), playground RAG function (E002 + branches + AC gate), separate-process decoupling (HLR-3 → E002), multi-tenant scoping (HLR-4), lightweight/resource profile (HLR-5 → E004). The \"Auto Rag functions\" criterion is intentionally not decomposed — a documented Staff Engineer Input override (decoupled to RHAISTRAT-7824), correctly recorded in the overrides section rather than silently dropped.\n88\t\n89\tBoth strategy Open Questions are explicitly resolved: scoping model confirmed as per-namespace in the E001 decision record (with the Auto Rag per-instance consumer decoupled), and migration path captured as HLR-6 portability ACs. All six Staff Engineer Input corrections and directions are carried forward (overrides section items 1–6 map one-to-one to the staff input paragraphs). Conditional branches cover all three bounded outcomes named in the staff input (operator-based, BFF-managed, bring-your-own). Strategy-level risks (RHAISTRAT-7164 release-commitment risk, branch C degraded outcome, branch B lifecycle risk, scoping assumption) are carried into Health Warnings and reflected in branch ACs.\n90\t\n91\tDocs-authoring check: the available strategy file contains no Prerequisites & Process Gates section declaring Documentation Support, and the DAG Justification records the reasoning for no docs-authoring epic — consistent with the available input (one minor re-verification note below, stemming from the missing attachment).\n92\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8116686d1268a87c","agent_id":"a2f086175853eb341","session_id":"9929ed70-9f3a-43fc-bc5e-5275c2fe8952","uuid":"86f0d3f3-91d7-4399-a154-6d42957ef9b1","timestamp":"2026-10-26