{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6ec6093eb44edc9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition: RHAISTRAT-8004 — Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio\n10\t\n11\tRHOAI must provide a default pgvector-backed vector store available out-of-the-box so that Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows keep functioning when inline providers are removed from the OGX (Llama Stack) distribution (RHAISTRAT-7474). Per Staff Engineer Input, the remote pgvector vector_io provider already exists in the OGX distribution and is largely reusable; the majority of the work is infrastructure and provisioning of the PostgreSQL/pgvector database, whose mechanism depends on the delivery timing of RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization). Auto Rag is decoupled from this strategy (moved to RHAISTRAT-7824's scope).\n12\t\n13\t## Epic List\n14\t\n15\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n16\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Provisioning approach go/no-go: operator-based vs BFF-managed vs bring-your-own PostgreSQL | Investigation | Dashboard Crimson Scrum | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Remote pgvector vector_io provider wiring for Gen AI Studio playgrounds | Implementation | Dashboard Crimson Scrum | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch A) | Implementation (branch A) | Dashboard Crimson Scrum | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (branch B) | Implementation (branch B) | Dashboard Crimson Scrum | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E003 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration support for Gen AI Studio RAG (branch C) | Implementation (branch C) | Dashboard Crimson Scrum | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | pgvector benchmark and resource sizing validation for the default vector store | Implementation | Dashboard Crimson Scrum | P1 |\n23\t\n24\t`epic_count` counts all epics including branches: 6 (3 main-plan epics + 3 conditional branch epics; exactly one branch epic is executed).\n25\t\n26\t## Dependency DAG\n27\t\n28\t```mermaid\n29\tgraph TD\n30\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001<br/>Investigation: provisioning go/no-go\"]\n31\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E002<br/>pgvector provider wiring\"]\n32\t    E004[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E004<br/>benchmark and sizing\"]\n33\t    BA[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003<br/>operator-based provisioning (branch A)\"]\n34\t    BB[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003<br/>BFF-managed provisioning (branch B)\"]\n35\t    BC[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E003<br/>bring-your-own PostgreSQL (branch C)\"]\n36\t    E001 --> BA\n37\t    E001 --> BB\n38\t    E001 --> BC\n39\t    E002 --> E004\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\tBranch epics are conditional alternatives, not main-plan nodes; they are excluded from the main-plan critical path. Main-plan critical path length: 2 (RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 → RHAISTRAT-8004-E004).\n43\t\n44\t## DAG Justification\n45\t\n46\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n47\t|------|------|-----------|\n48\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 → RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The go/no-go decision determines whether the operator-based provisioning epic exists at all (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing). Bounded outcome (≤3), so a conditional branch epic is emitted with `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. |\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 → RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Same gate; branch B is selected only if operator-based provisioning is unavailable and the BFF-managed alternative is judged sound. |\n50\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 → RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Same gate; branch C is the degraded fallback (customer-supplied PostgreSQL) if neither self-provisioning approach is viable for 3.5. |\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 → RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Rule 12 (framework/library → consumer) | The benchmark exercises the pgvector provider wiring delivered by the provider-wiring implementation; it cannot start meaningfully before that surface exists. |\n52\t| No edge RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 → RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (deliberate) | Rule 13 (configuration references are AC gates, not edges) | The provider wiring is identical under all three provisioning outcomes; only the connection-details source differs, and a connection string is a configuration reference. The coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on the wiring epic (\"verified against an instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach\"), not a DAG edge — the wiring proceeds in parallel with the decision. |\n53\t| No edge between RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 and any branch epic (deliberate) | Rule 13 | The provisioned database endpoint/connection string is a configuration reference consumed at execution time; the branch epics surface it and the wiring epic consumes it via an AC gate. No onboarding-chain epics exist (Staff Engineer Input: existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector; no Konflux onboarding, no custom image build), so Rules 5–10 ordering does not apply. |\n54\t| No docs-authoring epic | Rule 11 n/a | The strategy declares no Documentation Support requirement and Staff Engineer Input requests no new doc topics; user-facing configuration guidance for the bring-your-own outcome is scope inside that branch epic, not substantive new content authoring. |\n55\t\n56\t## Conditional Branches\n57\t\n58\tRHAISTRAT-8004-E001 resolves one gating unknown with three bounded outcomes; exactly one branch epic is executed:\n59\t\n60\t- **Branch A — operator-based self-provisioning**: RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) lands in time for RHOAI 3.5. PostgreSQL lifecycle management lives in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer, following the model-registry-operator pattern. Preferred long-term shape.\n61\t- **Branch B — BFF-managed provisioning**: RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time; the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured (the staff-endorsed alternative to forcing customers to bring their own database), with explicit lifecycle design attention.\n62\t- **Branch C — bring-your-own PostgreSQL**: neither self-provisioning approach is viable for 3.5; customers configure the OGXServer CR spec with their own PostgreSQL connection details. Degraded outcome — see Health Warnings.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n65\t\n66\tHLR identifiers are derived from the strategy's top-level Acceptance Criteria and Staff Engineer Input (the full strategy attachment with the authored HLR table was not available — see Health Warnings).\n67\t\n68\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n69\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n70\t| HLR-1 | P0 | A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure | RHAISTRAT-8004-E001; RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E003 (conditional) | The go/no-go investigation selects the mechanism; exactly one branch epic delivers the provisioning. Branch C only partially satisfies this HLR (customer-supplied database) — see Health Warnings. |\n71\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002, plus the selected provisioning branch epic | Provider wiring is branch-independent; end-to-end function requires the selected branch's provisioned instance (AC gate on the wiring epic). The strategy-level \"Auto Rag functions\" criterion is removed per Staff Engineer Input (decoupled to RHAISTRAT-7824). |\n72\t| HLR-3 | P0 | The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (not an inline provider) | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Remote pgvector vector_io provider configuration; the inline Milvus provider path is not used. |\n73\t| HLR-4 | P1 | The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (bundled); RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 / BRANCH-C-E003 | Bundled with the P0 wiring: per-namespace scoping is implemented in the same OGXServer/provider configuration surface the P0 provider wiring edits; a split would double-touch the same configuration code. The provisioning branches carry the instance-side scoping inherently (they are P0 epics, so no priority collapse occurs there). |\n74\t| HLR-5 | P1 | The default vector store is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments: benchmark within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline; resource sizing starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference | RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Dev-experimentation scope per Staff Engineer Input — no strict latency SLAs; flat scan (no index) is sufficient for typical playground document sets. |\n75\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Migration path / portability: the design must allow consuming a platform-provided shared PostgreSQL (RHOAI 3.6) and point production-scale vector workloads at external vector databases | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 wiring: the portability design (abstracted connection-details source over the existing multi-backend remote vector store implementation) edits the same configuration surface; a split would double-touch the same files. |\n76\t\n77\tEvery HLR is covered; no orphaned requirements. The two strategy Open Questions are resolved as follows: the scoping-model question (per-namespace vs per-instance) is confirmed as per-namespace inside the go/no-go decision record (Auto Rag, the potential per-instance consumer, is decoupled); the migration-path question is covered by HLR-6 as a portability acceptance criterion on the wiring epic rather than separate work, because the remote vector store implementation is already provider-configurable (Milvus, PGVector, Qdrant per the ogx-distribution architecture context).\n78\t\n79\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n80\t\n81\tThe Staff Engineer Input section is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated strategy; the following overrides shaped this decomposition:\n82\t\n83\t1. **Auto Rag removed from scope** — validation covers Gen AI Studio Playground RAG workflows only; Auto Rag/pgvector compatibility moves to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). The strategy-level \"Auto Rag functions with the default vector store\" acceptance criterion is not decomposed here.\n84\t2. **Corrected \"OGX auto-provisions PostgreSQL\"** — OGX defaults to SQLite for application storage; the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite when creating OGXServer instances. Introducing PostgreSQL for vector storage is new work, which is why the provisioning epics exist.\n85\t3. **Eliminated the custom image build and onboarding chain** — `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` already includes pgvector, the model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it, and the image environment variable is already present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs. No `repo-onboarding`, `konflux-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are generated; the strategy's 1–2 sprint image-onboarding estimate is eliminated.\n86\t4. **Vector storage decoupled from OGX application storage** — the pgvector vector_io provider configuration is separate from `storage.kv.type`/`storage.sql.type` (which remain SQLite). No vector-plus-application storage consolidation and no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL upgrade path are in scope.\n87\t5. **Performance scope reduced to dev experimentation** — order-of-magnitude comparison against the inline Milvus baseline instead of latency SLAs; open questions about latency thresholds, corpus size ranges, and index type selection are removed (flat scan is sufficient).\n88\t6. **Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC deferred to RHOAI 3.6** — captured as a portability acceptance criterion on the provider-wiring epic, not as an epic in this strategy.\n89\t\n90\t## Component Mapping\n91\t\n92\t- All epics use the canonical component **Gen AI Studio** (the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF and its provisioning surfaces). No **OGX Core** (ogx-distribution) code epic is generated: the ogx-distribution architecture context (`ogx-distribution.md`) shows the remote PGVector vector_io backend is already shipped (Milvus / PGVector / Qdrant remote backends; inline providers stripped), and Staff Engineer Input confirms the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable — the distribution-side scope resolves to configuration emitted by the gen-ai BFF.\n93\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 spans the dashboard operator layer delivered by RHAISTRAT-7164 (canonical component \"AI Core Dashboard\" territory); **Gen AI Studio** is assigned as the single most-relevant canonical name because the provisioning is Gen AI Studio-specific.\n94\t- Teams: the strategy's Impacted Teams table is in the unavailable attachment; all epics are assigned to **Dashboard Crimson Scrum**, inferred from the strategy's `dashboard-crimson-scrum` label. Branch A may shift to the operator/modularization team that owns the RHOAI-controlled operator layer once RHAISTRAT-7164 lands — verify at planning.\n95\t\n96\t## Health Warnings\n97\t\n98\t- **Missing strategy attachment**: the full strategy (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md` attachment — Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table, authored High Level Requirements with priorities, Dependencies table, Non-Functional Requirements) was not available in this workspace. HLRs and priorities above were derived from the strategy's top-level Acceptance Criteria and Staff Engineer Input; team assignments were inferred from the strategy label. Re-verify the HLR numbering and team mapping against the attachment before execution.\n99\t- **Release-commitment risk**: per Staff Engineer Input, this strategy cannot be committed to a specific release until RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) delivers. The go/no-go investigation plus conditional branches absorb the uncertainty, but the RHOAI 3.5 date should be treated as at-risk while RHAISTRAT-7164 is open.\n100\t- **Branch C is a degraded outcome**: bring-your-own PostgreSQL only partially satisfies HLR-1 — the strategy-level success criterion \"no additional manual setup steps\" is not met, and unblocking RHAISTRAT-7474 may need re-evaluation if branch C is selected. The Playground also largely abstracts OGX from users, so branch C must solve how customers surface connection details into the playground-created OGXServer instances.\n101\t- **Branch B lifecycle risk**: Staff Engineer Input explicitly flags that the gen-ai BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop — cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, and credential rotation need explicit design attention. Branch B's acceptance criteria enforce this; if design validation finds these unmanageable, fall back to branch C.\n102\t- **Scoping model assumption**: per-namespace isolation is the working assumption (Auto Rag decoupled). If a per-instance consumer re-enters scope (via RHAISTRAT-7824), revisit the provisioning and scoping design in that strategy rather than here.\n103\t\n104\t## Tiered Delivery\n105\t\n106\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing (RHAISTRAT-7164) is handled by conditional branches rather than a linear Tier 1/Tier 2 split, because the 3.5 fallbacks (branch B, and degraded branch C) still deliver most or all of the out-of-the-box experience:\n107\t\n108\t- **Branch-independent work (runs regardless of the gate)**: RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (provider wiring — identical under all outcomes) and RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 (benchmark and sizing — exercises the wired pgvector path). These deliver partial value and de-risk the integration while the go/no-go is pending.\n109\t- **Gate-dependent work**: exactly one of the three provisioning branch epics, selected by RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.\n110\t\n111\tThis structure also unblocks RHAISTRAT-7474 (inline provider removal) as soon as the selected branch completes, since the default vector store is then available at the platform level.\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8116686d1268a87c","agent_id":"a2f086175853eb341","session_id":"9929ed70-9f3a-43fc-bc5e-5275c2fe8952","uuid":"6af4aff3-1eda-41cf-8f6b-87d5b63677ba","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:41:58.962Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6ec6093eb44edc9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
